cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software or its results, please cite it using the metadata below."
type: software
title: "kwave-power-law-attenuation-calibration"
abstract: >-
  Reproducible numerical and analytical audit of power-law acoustic attenuation
  in k-Wave (MATLAB and CUDA), reconciling near-y=1 anomalies through exact
  fractional-Laplacian PDE theory under standard interior-source 1-D benchmarks
  (180 us, both p_max and p_min recorded) and 3-D controls. Parameter transfer
  from linear literature attenuation at 0.5 MHz reduces y=2 error from -50.24%
  to -1.14%. Near y=1, default dispersion follows the exact Treeby-Cox
  denominator 1 - (y+1)*a0*c0*tan(pi*y/2)*w^(y-1): y=1.01 yields a -57.37%
  attenuation deficit (-57.58% analytical) and +54.8% superluminal phase-speed
  shift (cp=4798 vs 3100 m/s), while y=0.98 yields +190.45% attenuation (+201.51%
  analytical, cp=1819 m/s). At y=0.99, effective stiffness M(k0)=-0.3505 < 0
  renders the wave equation Hadamard-elliptic, causing unconditional blow-up
  in both 1-D (-9.4e301 Pa) and 3-D (~1e30 Pa) across all timesteps. k-Wave's
  fitPowerLawParams restores amplitude near y=1 (-0.8% to -1.4%) but retains
  +55% to +104% phase-speed distortion; only y=2 (or y=1 with no_dispersion
  when phase dispersion is unneeded and backend support is verified) avoids
  both pitfalls.
